From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 15:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: take the DCB rewrite table down to the switch

DCB has carried a rewrite table since the app table grew its inverse: an entry
whose priority is the key and whose protocol is the value a packet of that
priority leaves with, written with `dcb rewr` and implemented by sparx5 and
lan966x. DSA implements neither half of the pair, so an entry aimed at a switch
port falls through to dcb_setrewr, lands in the software list, is reported as
success and reaches nothing. The switch goes on emitting whatever PCP or DSCP
the frame arrived with, and the priority a bridged network was classified into
cannot be put back on the wire for the next hop to read.

Add port_add/del_dscp_rewr and port_add/del_pcp_rewr and the two dcbnl ops that
reach them. The protocol bound comes from the selector - 64 for DSCP, and 16
for PCP because DCB carries the DEI bit above the PCP in that field - and the
priority is bounded at IEEE_8021QAZ_MAX_TCS, which is where the mask maps stop
reading.

A priority has one value, but the table can hold two entries for one priority:
`dcb rewr replace` adds the new entry before removing the old, so refusing the
second would break it. What the switch is told is therefore recomputed from the
mask map after every change - the surviving value if the priority still has
one, a clear if it does not - which is why both halves of a pair are required
before either is called, and why the delete op takes no protocol: by the time
it runs, the priority has nothing left to name.

A switch that holds one map for all its ports is served by the fan-out the app
entries already use, given the pair of table calls to make instead of choosing
between dcb_ieee_setapp and dcb_ieee_delapp from a bool. It is keyed on the two
flags that are already there: the flag says where the switch keeps its priority
map, and hardware that holds the classification result and the value to write
back in one entry has one answer for both directions.

A switch carrying neither op answers -EOPNOTSUPP, where the entry used to
reach the software list and be reported as done: installing the ops takes
away the dcb_setrewr fallback the core picks while they are absent.

Not sent to netdev: no in-tree DSA driver has these ops, so upstream would be
taking a switch API nothing in the kernel implements - the same reason the PCP
app entry patch beside it stays here.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
---
--- a/include/net/dsa.h
+++ b/include/net/dsa.h
@@ -995,6 +995,13 @@
 				     u8 prio);
 	int	(*port_del_pcp_prio)(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port, u8 pcp,
 				     u8 prio);
+	int	(*port_add_dscp_rewr)(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port, u8 prio,
+				      u8 dscp);
+	int	(*port_del_dscp_rewr)(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port,
+				      u8 prio);
+	int	(*port_add_pcp_rewr)(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port, u8 prio,
+				     u8 pcp);
+	int	(*port_del_pcp_rewr)(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port, u8 prio);
 	int	(*port_set_apptrust)(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port,
 				     const u8 *sel, int nsel);
 	int	(*port_get_apptrust)(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port, u8 *sel,
--- a/net/dsa/user.c
+++ b/net/dsa/user.c
@@ -2250,20 +2250,18 @@
